##
   `const` and `mut var` are two qualifiers on the same binding and genuinely
   contradict each other, so pairing them is rejected. `mut T` is not in that
   category -- it qualifies the type, not the binding -- so a const binding may
   name a mutable type. "Constant name, mutable storage."

   The binding stays constant: `x = 2` is refused with a constant-variable
   error, not a mutability one.

   Scope note: a const binding can only ever hold a scalar, because
   `is_compile_time_literal` restricts its initializer and every literal except
   an array or null is a scalar. So there is currently no way to write through a
   const binding -- the point of the rule is that the type qualifier is no
   longer reported as if it were a binding qualifier.
##
const var x: mut i32 = 1i32;
const var y: i32 = 1i32;

func read_them(): i32 {
  var a: i32 = x;
  var b: i32 = y;
  return a + b;
}
